Audit findings and actions
- All-feature Rustdoc JSON surfaced public `RequestResponseFuture::is_timeout_at`
  on the Core API. Source search found callers only in the Core request/reply
  registry; the Java 5.5.1 API has `isTimeout()` and no explicit-clock method.
  Changed the helper to `pub(super)` and added a compile-fail doctest proving it
  is not externally callable. The Phase 15 matrix records the owner-only clock
  adapter disposition.
- A nearby Java-worktree method, `acquireCountDownLatch`, was checked before
  implementation. It is absent from pinned commit
  `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9` and from the Maven 5.5.1 artifact;
  it exists only in the current Java worktree at `2238256de1d227a4384ba969dfa31473187b4d08`.
  An initial fixture compilation correctly failed to resolve it. The temporary
  candidate changes were reverted; no Java-oracle/API mismatch remains from
  that probe.
- Pinned `RequestResponseFuture.java` SHA-256:
  `08f2a1eec7e492d95fd9502e3b942960df948674182de5632b7b02d0514d95d4`.
- Code commit: `aa4ce064` (`fix(core): keep request timeout clock helper
  internal`). Documentation and this log are committed separately.

